––– comment –––
Columnar analyzers with exclude filters (found in the #5006 review).
10000 distinct ints give generic-packed blocks, so NOT IN with two values goes through the linear values analyzer.
10000 strings of one length give const-length blocks whose last subblock is partial; under a case-sensitive
collation the raw string analyzer is used, and threads=1 leaves no rowid boundary to clip rows past the end.
––– input –––
sql() { mysql -h0 -P9306 --skip-table --batch --raw --skip-column-names -e "$1"; }
––– output –––
––– block: ../base/start-searchd –––
––– input –––
{ echo "CREATE TABLE t (s STRING engine='columnar', n INT engine='columnar');"; for b in $(seq 0 9); do echo "INSERT INTO t VALUES $(for i in $(seq $((b*1000)) $((b*1000+999))); do printf "(%d,'v%04d',%d)," $((i+1)) $i $i; done | sed 's/,$//');"; done; echo "FLUSH RAMCHUNK t;"; } | mysql -h0 -P9306 && echo ready
––– output –––
ready
––– input –––
sql "SELECT COUNT(*), SUM(n) FROM t WHERE n NOT IN (1,2) /*+ ColumnarScan(n) */; SHOW META LIKE 'index';" | tr '\t' ' '
––– output –––
9998 49994997
index n:ColumnarScan (100%)
––– input –––
sql "SET collation_connection=binary; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t WHERE s != 'v0001' OPTION threads=1 /*+ ColumnarScan(s) */; SHOW META LIKE 'index'; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t WHERE s NOT IN ('v0001','v0002') OPTION threads=1 /*+ ColumnarScan(s) */;" | tr '\t' ' '
––– output –––
9999
index s:ColumnarScan (100%)
9998
––– input –––
sql "DROP TABLE t;"
––– output –––
––– comment –––
Issue #5047: when every row of a block passes (here a constant string, so a const block of its hash), the analyzer
filled the row ids of a subblock four at a time and restarted the last n%4 from the first one. Only the last subblock
of a chunk can be partial: 8197 rows leave 5. Intersected with the id analyzer the filter stopped after the first row
of that subblock (8193 instead of 8197); on its own it returned id 8193 twice and lost 8197. One INSERT keeps
rowid = id-1; chunks under 8192 rows get no analyzers.
––– input –––
{ echo "CREATE TABLE t2 (s STRING) engine='columnar';"; echo "INSERT INTO t2 (id,s) VALUES $(printf "(%d,'a')," $(seq 1 8197) | sed 's/,$//');"; echo "FLUSH RAMCHUNK t2;"; } | mysql -h0 -P9306 && echo ready
––– output –––
ready
––– input –––
sql "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t2 WHERE s='a' AND id>0 OPTION threads=1 /*+ ColumnarScan(s,id) */; SHOW META LIKE 'index';" | tr '\t' ' '
––– output –––
8197
index id:ColumnarScan (100%), $s_HASH:ColumnarScan (100%)
––– input –––
sql "SELECT id FROM t2 WHERE s='a' AND id>0 ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 5 OPTION threads=1 /*+ NO_ColumnarScan(id) */; SHOW META LIKE 'index';" | tr '\t' ' ' | paste -sd' ' -
––– output –––
8197 8196 8195 8194 8193 index $s_HASH:ColumnarScan (100%)
––– input –––
sql "DROP TABLE t2;"
––– output –––
––– comment –––
Issue #5057: a columnar filter left to per-row checks (its ColumnarScan disabled) next to a ColumnarScan of another
attribute. The block min/max test of the scan still had that filter, but the columnar library reads min/max only for
the attributes of the scan filters, so the filter saw min=max=0 (c, before p) or a slot past the end (z, after p):
c=1 returned 0 rows, z=1 lost some or all of them.
––– input –––
{ echo "CREATE TABLE t3 (c INT, p BIGINT, z INT) engine='columnar';"; echo "INSERT INTO t3 (id,c,p,z) VALUES $(seq 1 8200 | awk '{printf "%s(%d,1,%d,%d)", (NR>1?",":""), $1, $1, $1%2}');"; echo "FLUSH RAMCHUNK t3;"; } | mysql -h0 -P9306 && echo ready
––– output –––
ready
––– input –––
sql "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t3 WHERE p>0 AND c=1 OPTION threads=1 /*+ ColumnarScan(p) NO_ColumnarScan(c) NO_SecondaryIndex(c) */; SHOW META LIKE 'index';" | tr '\t' ' '
––– output –––
8200
index p:ColumnarScan (100%)
––– input –––
sql "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t3 WHERE p>0 AND z=1 OPTION threads=1 /*+ ColumnarScan(p) NO_ColumnarScan(z) NO_SecondaryIndex(z) */; SHOW META LIKE 'index';" | tr '\t' ' '
––– output –––
4100
index p:ColumnarScan (100%)
––– input –––
sql "DROP TABLE t3;"
––– output –––
––– comment –––
Also #5057: the kept filters kept their positions from before that removal, while the columnar library reports the
analyzers it created by position in the shortened list. With x=5 on a secondary index first, y NOT BETWEEN (the int
analyzer refuses excluded ranges, so y stays a per-row filter) took the mark of the z analyzer and was dropped: id 105
came back, 100 rows instead of 99, no hints needed. Without y, z kept a zero estimate and led the intersection.
––– input –––
{ echo "CREATE TABLE t4 (x INT, y INT, z INT) engine='columnar';"; echo "INSERT INTO t4 (id,x,y,z) VALUES $(seq 1 10000 | awk '{printf "%s(%d,%d,%d,%d)", (NR>1?",":""), $1, $1%100, $1, $1}');"; echo "FLUSH RAMCHUNK t4;"; } | mysql -h0 -P9306 && echo ready
––– output –––
ready
––– input –––
sql "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t4 WHERE x=5 AND y NOT BETWEEN 100 AND 200 AND z>=0 OPTION threads=1; SHOW META LIKE 'index';" | tr '\t' ' '
––– output –––
99
index x:SecondaryIndex (100%), z:ColumnarScan (100%)
––– input –––
sql "SELECT id FROM t4 WHERE x=5 AND y NOT BETWEEN 100 AND 200 AND z>=0 ORDER BY id ASC LIMIT 3 OPTION threads=1 /*+ SecondaryIndex(x) ColumnarScan(y,z) */;" | paste -sd' ' -
––– output –––
5 205 305
––– input –––
sql "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t4 WHERE x=5 AND z>=0 OPTION threads=1; SHOW META LIKE 'index';" | tr '\t' ' '
––– output –––
100
index x:SecondaryIndex (100%), z:ColumnarScan (100%)
––– input –––
sql "DROP TABLE t4;"
